.\" Manual page for agentcracker.
.\" View in the source tree with: man -l man/agentcracker.1
.TH AGENTCRACKER 1 "November 2026" "agentcracker 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agentcracker, acr \- run coding agents in disposable Firecracker microVMs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agentcracker
.RB [ \-\-allow
.IR HOST ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-no\-net ]
.RB [ \-\-console ]
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.RB [ \-\-branch
.IR NAME ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B agentcracker run
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B agentcracker ls
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B agentcracker show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker log
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker diff
.RB [ \-\-ignored ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker apply
.RB [ \-\-ignored ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker branch
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker resume
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ session ]
.RI [ command " [" args ...]]
.br
.B agentcracker shell
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B agentcracker wait
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ session ...]
.br
.B agentcracker stop
.RI [ session ...]
.br
.B agentcracker rm
.IR session ...
.br
.B agentcracker refresh
.RB [ \-\-background ]
.br
.B agentcracker cache
.RB [ \-\-drop
.RI [ dir ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B acr
is installed as a short alias for
.BR agentcracker .
.PP
.B agentcracker
runs a command \(em typically a coding agent such as
.BR claude (1)
or
.BR codex (1)
\(em inside a throwaway Firecracker microVM, so that the agent can edit,
build, test and install freely without being able to touch the host.
.PP
The VM sees:
.IP \(bu 2
the current directory, at the same path, as a read-only snapshot with a
writable layer on top: nothing the agent does reaches the real directory
until it is applied;
.IP \(bu 2
the host's own
.IR /usr ,
the toolchains found on
.B PATH
under
.I $HOME
(rustup, nvm, the agent CLI itself, ...), and a small allowlisted part of
.IR /etc ,
all as read-only snapshots, so the agent finds the same tools as the user;
.IP \(bu 2
its own
.IR $HOME ,
seeded with the agent's settings and credentials but otherwise empty;
.IP \(bu 2
no network except HTTPS to allowed hosts, through a proxy on the host
(the Anthropic API by default).
.PP
The agent runs as the invoking user, in a separate guest kernel.  The VM
starts in well under a second once the
.I /usr
snapshot exists (the first run builds it, which takes tens of seconds).
.PP
The first session in a directory also snapshots the whole project,
including
.IR .git .
For a large tree such as a Linux kernel checkout that takes about ten
seconds on btrfs or XFS, where file data is shared by reflink, and much
longer, using as much disk again as the tree, on filesystems without
reflinks.  Later sessions reuse the snapshot, or add a small layer
holding just what changed since, and start in under a second.  Build
output the agent does not need can be left out with
.B exclude
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.PP
Each run creates a
.IR session :
a directory holding the VM's writable disk, the project snapshot it
started from, a record of what changed, the network log and the agent's
transcripts.  When the command exits the VM powers off; on a terminal,
agentcracker then lists the changes and offers a review menu (see
.BR "REVIEW MENU" ).
A session can be resumed, shelled into, diffed and applied at any time
until it is removed.
.SH COMMANDS
A first argument that is not one of the subcommands below is the command
to run.  Use
.B run
for a command whose name collides with a subcommand.
.TP
.BI run " command " \fR[\fIargs\fR...]
Create a new session for the current directory and run
.I command
in it.  On a terminal the command gets a terminal of its own (sized like
and resized with the host's) and the review menu follows; otherwise its
standard input, output and error are plain pipes and a one-line summary
of the changes is printed instead.  The exit status is the command's.
.IP
The current directory must not be
.I /
or an ancestor of
.IR $HOME .
.TP
.B ls
List all sessions, oldest first: id, age, status
.RB ( running ,
.BR exited ,
.BI "exit " N\fR,
.BR applied ,
.B stopped
when its host process died,
.BR crashed ,
.BR failed ),
change counts (+added ~modified \-deleted), directory and command.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
a JSON array instead (see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" ).
.TP
.BR show " [\fIsession\fR]"
Everything recorded about a session: command, times, status and boot
count, VM shape and network policy, the list of changes, each host it
connected to or was refused (the agent chose those names: allow one only
if the work really needs it), the agent's transcripts, disk use, and the size of
the directory's build cache.
.TP
.BR log " [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fIsession\fR]"
The session's activity as one timeline, in local time: every connection
attempt the agent made through the egress proxy, with its verdict
.RB ( ALLOW ,
.BR DENY ,
\&...), every test kernel it booted with
.B acrboot
(with the outcome), and every tool call in the agent's Claude Code transcripts,
subagents' included: shell commands, files read and edited, fetched URLs,
web searches.  Searches run on Anthropic's servers and are marked as
never seen by the proxy.  The proxy sees only
.IR host : port
\(em URLs and contents are inside TLS.  With
.BR \-f ,
a running session's events are printed as they happen: network requests
at once, tool calls and boots within a couple of seconds (they are read
out of its VM, through the same connection as
.BR shell ).
With
.BR \-\-json ,
one object per event.  All text is the agent's and is shown escaped.
.TP
.BR diff " [\fB\-\-ignored\fR] [\fIsession\fR]"
Show the session's changes as a unified diff against the current host
files, through
.B $PAGER
on a terminal.  Changes to files that were also changed on the host are
marked
.BR CONFLICT .
Changes git ignores are left out unless
.B \-\-ignored
is given (see
.BR "APPLYING CHANGES" ).
.TP
.BR apply " [\fB\-\-ignored\fR] [\fIsession\fR]"
Write the session's changes into its directory on the host; with
.BR \-\-ignored ,
those git ignores too.  If the host
changed any of the same files since the session started, apply asks
whether to skip those, overwrite them or cancel; without a terminal it
refuses and changes nothing.  Changes the host already has are skipped,
so applying twice is harmless.  See
.BR "APPLYING CHANGES" .
.TP
.BR branch " [\fIsession\fR]"
If the agent made git commits, import them into the host repository as
branch
.BI agentcracker/ session
(replaced by a later import), or as the session's
.B \-\-branch
name (fast-forward only).
The current branch, index and work tree are not touched.
.TP
.BR resume " [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIsession\fR] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...]]"
Boot the session again on the same writable disk, so earlier changes, the
guest
.I $HOME
and anything installed are still there, and run
.I command
(by default the session's original command).  Changes accumulate across
boots.
.B \-\-allow
adds hosts to the session's allowlist;
.B \-\-no\-net
turns its network off.
.TP
.BR shell " [\fIsession\fR]"
Like
.B resume
with the user's login shell as the command.  If the session is running
(in another terminal, or in the background), the shell opens inside that
same VM instead, beside the session's command and seeing its work as it
happens; several can be open at once.  Leaving the shell leaves the
session running; when the session's command exits, the VM shuts down and
such shells end with it.  Their changes are part of the session.
.TP
.BR wait " [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fIsession\fR...]"
Wait until each session has ended, printing
.I "ID status"
(or a JSON object) as each one does.  The exit status is the command's
for a single session; for several, 0 if every one exited 0.  Interrupting
.B wait
leaves the sessions running.
.TP
.BR stop " [\fIsession\fR...]"
End running sessions cleanly: the command gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 5
seconds later if it is still there, and the session exports its changes
and ends as usual, so it can still be diffed, applied or resumed.  Waits
for each session to end (up to 30 seconds).  A running session cannot be
removed with
.BR rm ;
stop it first.
.TP
.BI rm " session" \fR...
Delete sessions and everything recorded for them.  The project directory
is never touched.
.TP
.BR refresh " [" \-\-background ]
Rebuild the
.I /usr
and toolchain snapshots now.  Snapshots are refreshed automatically when
they go stale (the toolchain snapshot before the next boot, the
.I /usr
snapshot in the background while the old one keeps being used), so this
is rarely needed.
.TP
.BR cache " [\fB\-\-drop\fR [\fIdir\fR]]"
List every project's build cache: its size, when a session last used it,
and its directory.  With
.BR \-\-drop ,
delete the build cache of
.I dir
(default: the current directory), so its next session starts with an
empty
.IR ~/.cache ;
a cache in use by a running session cannot be dropped.  See
.BR "BUILD CACHE" .
.SS Naming sessions
A
.I session
argument is a session id or any unique prefix of one.  When it is
omitted, the latest session for the current directory is used.
.SH OPTIONS
These come before the command (for a new session) or after
.BR resume .
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " host"
Also allow HTTPS to
.IR host .
.BI *. domain
allows every subdomain of
.I domain
(but not
.I domain
itself);
.IB host : port
allows a port other than 443.
.B "\-\-allow \(aq*\(aq"
allows HTTPS to any host, which gives up most of the network sandbox
(the session banner says so).  May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-net
No network at all.
.TP
.B \-\-console
Show the guest kernel console (for debugging).
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Start the session in the background: print its id and return at once.
The command gets no input and no terminal; its standard output and error
go to the session's
.I stdout
and
.I stderr
files.  There is no review menu: the session ends as an ordinary kept
session.  With
.BR \-\-json ,
print
.B {\(dqid\(dq: ..., \(dqdir\(dq: ...}
instead of the bare id.
.TP
.BI \-\-branch " NAME"
In a git repository, start the agent on a new branch
.I NAME
at the current
.B HEAD
(the branch must not exist yet; your own checkout is not changed), and
tell it to commit there.
.B branch
then imports the agent's commits as
.I NAME
instead of
.BI agentcracker/ session\fR,
and only ever fast-forwards it: an import of rewritten commits, or into a
.I NAME
that moved or is checked out, is refused.  New sessions only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH REVIEW MENU
When a session's command exits and agentcracker is on a terminal, it
prints the change list and a menu.  Each choice is a single letter
followed by Enter:
.TP
.B d
diff: show the changes, then return to the menu.
.TP
.B a
apply: write the changes to the host (resolving conflicts if there are
any), then ask whether to keep the session.
.TP
.B b
branch: import the agent's git commits (offered only if it made some).
.TP
.B i
ignored: include the changes git ignores in the list, the diff and apply,
or leave them out again (offered only if there are some).
.TP
.B s
shell: open a shell inside the session; exiting it returns to the menu.
.TP
.BR K " (default)"
keep for later: leave the session as it is and apply nothing.  Use
.BR "agentcracker apply" ,
.B resume
or
.B rm
on it later.
.TP
.B x
discard: delete the session (after confirmation if it has unapplied
changes).
.PP
Nothing reaches the project directory unless
.B a
(or the
.B apply
command) is chosen.
.SH BUILD CACHE
Each project directory has one persistent disk, mounted as
.I ~/.cache
in every session of that project, so build caches survive from one
session to the next: ccache, sccache, Go's build cache and pip keep
theirs there already, and an out-of-tree build can go there too
.RB ( "make O=~/.cache/kbuild" ).
It is not part of the project, so nothing in it is ever reviewed or
applied.
.PP
One running session uses a project's cache at a time; another session
of the same project meanwhile gets an empty
.I ~/.cache
of its own, and says so.  The host never mounts the cache: what an agent
leaves in it can mislead later sessions of the same project, never the
host.
.B "agentcracker cache \-\-drop"
starts it afresh, and
.B cache = false
in the configuration turns it off.
.SH BOOTING TEST KERNELS
With
.B boot = true
in the user's configuration, every session has an
.B acrboot
command, for an agent working on a Linux kernel to run the kernel it
built:
.PP
.EX
acrboot [\-\-cpus N] [\-\-mem SIZE] [\-\-timeout SECS] [\-\-append ARGS]
        [\-\-disk FILE]... [\-\-run CMD [\-\-dir DIR]] VMLINUX
.EE
.PP
The session's VM has nested virtualisation for this: the kernel boots in
a Firecracker microVM of its own inside the session's, and its serial
console is shown.  The host's KVM must allow it
.RB ( kvm_intel " or " kvm_amd
with
.BR nested=1 );
without
.B boot = true
the guest has no
.IR /dev/kvm ,
and the host hides VMX and SVM from it.
.B acrboot
exits 0 when the kernel shut the VM down, 1 on a kernel panic, 124 on a
timeout and 2 on an error.  The kernel must be the uncompressed ELF
.I vmlinux
(not a
.IR bzImage )
and built to boot under Firecracker:
.B "acrboot \-\-kconfig"
prints the options it needs, to merge with
.BR scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh .
End the boot with a reboot (for KUnit,
.BR kunit_shutdown=reboot ).
The agent learns all this from an
.B agentcracker\-acrboot
skill installed in the guest when booting is enabled (see
.BR AGENTS ).
Each
.B \-\-disk
is attached read-write, in place, the first as
.IR /dev/vda ,
which is the root device unless
.B \-\-append
sets
.BR root= .
.PP
.B \-\-run
.I CMD
boots the kernel on the session's own userspace instead: its root and
.I /usr
images, read-only, and a copy of
.I DIR
(default the current directory) at the same path, read-write and
discarded after.  Its init mounts
.IR /proc ,
.IR /sys ,
.IR /dev ,
tmpfs on
.I /tmp
and others, bpffs, debugfs, tracefs and cgroup2, brings up
.BR lo ,
and runs
.I CMD
as root with
.B sh \-c
in
.IR DIR ;
.B acrboot
then exits with its status (124 on a timeout, 125 if the VM ended
without it finishing).  For the BPF selftests:
.PP
.EX
acrboot \-\-timeout 3600 \-\-dir tools/testing/selftests/bpf \-\-run ./test_progs vmlinux
.EE
.PP
The test VM has no network, CPUs and memory out of the session's
(default 2 CPUs and 1G), and a timeout (default 600 seconds); it is
stopped a few seconds after a panic, and when the session ends.  Each
boot is recorded in the guest's
.IR ~/.local/state/acrboot/ :
a line in
.I boot.log
and its console (the first 4M, last 20 boots) as
.IR N.console .
These come out with the session's state when the VM stops, and each boot
appears in
.BR "agentcracker log" .
.PP
It is off by default and cannot be turned on by a project's
.IR .agentcracker.toml :
it gives the agent ring 0 in a VM, so the boundary becomes KVM and
Firecracker's device emulation facing a kernel the agent controls.
.SH APPLYING CHANGES
Each change is listed with a letter:
.B A
added,
.B M
modified (including permission-only changes),
.B D
deleted (a deleted directory shows how many entries it held).  Notes
after an entry mark
.B conflict
(the host copy changed since the session started),
.B "already on the host"
and files that
.B "run or configure tools on the host"
(Makefile, package.json, .envrc, .github/, newly executable files, ...),
which deserve review before applying.
.PP
In a git work tree, changes git ignores \(em build output such as
.IR *.o ,
.I *.ko
and
.IR build/ ,
by the project's
.I .gitignore
files \(em are left out: not listed, not in the diff and not applied,
only counted
.RI ( "+1624 ignored by git" ).
So a session that built the project reviews like one that did not.  The
rules are git's own, as they are on the host
.RB ( "git check-ignore" ):
the agent's edits to ignore files hide nothing, and tracked files are
never ignored.  If git ignores the project directory itself (it is inside
another repository that ignores it), nothing is.  Include them with
.B i
in the review menu, or
.BR "diff \-\-ignored" " and " "apply \-\-ignored" .
.PP
Apply writes each file to a temporary name and renames it into place,
never follows symlinks out of the project directory, drops setuid and
setgid bits, and never writes inside
.IR .git/ ;
the agent's commits are brought over with
.B branch
instead.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
.BR "ls \-\-json" ,
.BR "show \-\-json" ,
.BR "wait \-\-json" ,
.B "log \-\-json"
and
.B "\-\-detach \-\-json"
print JSON for scripts and orchestrators.  Every object carries
.BR \(dqformat\(dq:\ 1 ;
fields may be added within a format, but removing or changing one bumps
it.
.PP
.B ls
prints an array, and
.B wait
one object per line, of the short form:
.BR id ,
.BR dir ,
.B command
(an array),
.B status
(as in
.BR ls ),
.B exit_code
(or null),
.BR running ,
.B created
and
.B ended
(Unix times), and
.B changes
.RB ( added ,
.BR modified ,
.BR deleted ,
.BR inside_git ,
.B ignored
(changes git ignores, not in the other counts);
null before the first export).
.PP
.B show
adds
.BR boots ,
.BR runtime_secs ,
.BR error ,
.B vm
.RB ( vcpus ,
.BR memory_mib ,
.BR net ,
.BR allow ),
.B git
.RB ( base ,
.B agent_head
as
.BR {branch,\ commit} ,
.BR imported_branch ),
.BR applied_at ,
.B changes.files
(one object per change:
.BR path ,
.B status
.RB ( added ,
.BR modified ,
.BR deleted ),
.B type
.RB ( file ,
.BR symlink ,
.BR dir ,
.BR deleted ),
.B mode
(octal),
.BR conflict ,
.BR runs_on_host ,
.BR already_on_host ,
and for symlinks
.B target
and
.BR points_outside ),
.B network
(per host:
.BR host ,
.BR allowed ,
.BR denied ),
.B transcripts
.RB ( id ,
.BR messages ,
.BR last ,
.BR first_prompt ),
.B disk
.RB ( own ,
.B shared
bytes) and
.B paths
.RB ( session ,
.BR stdout ,
.BR stderr ,
.BR transcripts ).
.PP
.B log
prints one object per line per event:
.B time
(Unix time, with fractions),
.B kind
.RB ( net ,
or the tool's name in lower case:
.BR bash ,
.BR read ,
.BR edit ,
.BR webfetch ,
.BR websearch ,
\&...),
.B detail
(the target, command, file, URL or query),
.B verdict
(for
.BR net )
and
.BR server_side .
.PP
Strings that come from the guest (paths, symlink targets, host names,
transcript text) are the agent's: JSON escaping makes them safe to carry,
not safe to act on.  A program reading this output \(em especially an agent
\(em should treat them as untrusted data.
.SH AGENTS
Each session uses the profile of one agent, which decides the default
network allowlist, which of the agent's files are copied into the guest,
which environment variables are passed through, and which transcripts
.B show
and
.B log
read.  The profile is chosen from the command
.RB ( claude ,
.BR codex ,
also behind
.BR env ,
.BR npx ,
.B "sh \-c"
and the like), else from the
.B agent
config key, else Claude Code; it is recorded in the session, so
.B resume
and
.B shell
keep it.
.TP
.B claude
Allows
.BR anthropic.com ,
.B claude.ai
and
.B claude.com
and their subdomains.  Copies in
.IR ~/.claude/ 's
.BR settings.json ,
.BR CLAUDE.md ,
commands, agents, skills, plugins and output styles, this project's
transcripts and a
.I ~/.claude.json
pruned to this project; the login
.RI ( .credentials.json )
on every boot.  Keeps
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
Passes
.B ANTHROPIC_*
and
.BR CLAUDE_CODE_* .
.TP
.B codex
Allows
.B openai.com
and
.B chatgpt.com
and their subdomains.  Copies in
.IR ~/.codex/ 's
(or
.BR $CODEX_HOME 's,
when it is inside
.IR $HOME )
.BR config.toml ,
.BR AGENTS.md ,
.BR AGENTS.override.md ,
skills, agents, rules and themes; the login
.RI ( auth.json )
on every boot.  Never its
.IR secrets/ ,
.IR gateway_oauth.json ,
.I .credentials.json
(other credentials),
.IR memories/ ,
.I history.jsonl
or other sessions.  Keeps the session's rollouts
.RI ( sessions/ ).
Passes
.B OPENAI_*
and
.BR CODEX_* .
Log in on the host
.RB ( "codex login" );
the login is copied in.
.PP
For every agent, git gets only your identity
.RB ( user.name " and " user.email ,
written to the guest's
.I ~/.gitconfig
on every boot) and your global ignore file \(em never the rest of your
git configuration, which can hold passwords and tokens \(em a login
refreshed inside the guest is reported but never copied back, and files
that instruct agents
.RB ( CLAUDE.md ,
.BR AGENTS.md ,
.IR .codex/ ,
.IR .cursor/ ,
\&...) are flagged at review: every later agent run on the repository will
follow them.
.SS What the agent is told
On every boot, agentcracker adds a short section to the guest's copy of
the agent's user-level instructions file
.RI ( ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md ,
or Codex's
.I AGENTS.md
or
.IR AGENTS.override.md ):
its changes are reviewed before they reach the project directory, which
hosts the network allows (and that a refused one means asking the user),
that
.I ~/.cache
persists, and whether
.B acrboot
is available.  The section is marked, so the next boot replaces it; the
rest of the file is the user's.  With booting enabled an
.B agentcracker\-acrboot
skill is installed too.  Neither ever reaches the host.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from
.I ~/.config/agentcracker/config.toml
and then from
.I .agentcracker.toml
in the project directory.  List values from both files are combined.
.TP
.BR vcpus " = \fIN\fR"
vCPUs per VM (default: host CPUs, at most 8).
.TP
.BR memory " = \(dq\fISIZE\fR\(dq"
Guest memory, e.g. \(dq4G\(dq or \(dq2048M\(dq (default: half of host
RAM, between 1G and 8G).
.TP
.BR net " = true|false"
Network on or off (default on).
.TP
.BR allow " = [\fIhosts\fR]"
Extra allowed hosts, as for
.BR \-\-allow .
.TP
.BR exclude " = [\fIpatterns\fR]"
Paths left out of the project snapshot, gitignore style (a leading
.B /
anchors a pattern to the project root).
.TP
.BR exclude_usr " = [\fIpaths\fR]"
Extra paths under
.I /usr
to leave out of its snapshot.
.TP
.BR tools " = [\fIdirs\fR]", " no_tools" " = [\fIdirs\fR]"
Add or remove toolchain directories (beyond those found from
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BR home " = [\fIpaths\fR]"
Extra paths under
.I $HOME
copied into the guest on a session's first boot.
.TP
.BR state " = [\fIpaths\fR]"
Extra paths under the guest
.I $HOME
saved into the session on every exit.
.TP
.BR env " = [\fInames\fR]"
Extra environment variables passed into the guest (terminal, locale,
editor, pager and the agent profile's variables are passed by default).
.TP
.BR boot " = true|false"
Give sessions the
.B acrboot
command (default: false; see
.BR "BOOTING TEST KERNELS" ).
Only the user's configuration may set it.
.TP
.BR cache " = true|false"
Give sessions the project's persistent build cache as
.I ~/.cache
(default: true; see
.BR "BUILD CACHE" ).
.TP
.BR agent " = \(dq\fIname\fR\(dq"
The agent profile
.RB ( claude
or
.BR codex )
for commands that are not recognisably an agent, such as a shell
(default:
.BR claude ;
see
.BR AGENTS ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PAGER
Pager for
.BR diff .
.TP
.BR XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Base directories for sessions, cached images, configuration and sockets.
.TP
.B AGENTCRACKER_LIB
Directory holding
.BR acinit ,
.BR firecracker ,
.B vmlinux
and
.BR seccomp.bpf ,
overriding the location next to the binary.
.TP
.B AGENTCRACKER_TIMING
If set, print how long each startup and shutdown phase took.
.TP
.B AGENTCRACKER_FC_LOG
Firecracker's log level (default
.BR Error );
its log goes to the session's
.IR console.log .
.PP
Inside the guest,
.B AGENTCRACKER=1
and
.B AGENTCRACKER_SESSION
(the session id) are set, and the
.B HTTPS_PROXY
family points at the in-guest end of the egress proxy.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/agentcracker/sessions/ID/
One directory per session:
.I meta.json
(status, times, settings),
.I lower.img
and
.I lower\-delta.img
(the project snapshot, shared with the cache),
.I manifest.bin
(what the snapshot contained),
.I scratch.img
(the VM's writable disk),
.I changes.stream
(the changes, as exported on exit),
.I state/
(the agent's transcripts, and
.B acrboot
records in
.IR state/.local/state/acrboot/ ),
.I stdout
and
.I stderr
(output of sessions run with
.BR \-\-detach ),
.I net.log
and
.IR console.log .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/agentcracker/caches/KEY/
One directory per project with a build cache:
.I cache.img
(the sparse ext4 disk mounted as the guest's
.IR ~/.cache ),
.I dir
(the project directory) and
.IR lock .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/agentcracker/
Cached images:
.I usr/
and
.I tools/
snapshots,
.I base/
(the guest root and init),
.I lower/
(project snapshots; unused ones are dropped after a week).
.TP
.IR PREFIX /lib/agentcracker/
.B acinit
(the guest's init),
.BR firecracker ,
.B vmlinux
(the guest kernel) and
.B seccomp.bpf
(Firecracker's syscall filter).
.SH EXIT STATUS
For a new session,
.B resume
and
.BR shell :
the command's exit status (128 +
.I N
if it was killed by signal
.IR N ),
or 1 if the VM could not be started
.RB ( \-\-detach
exits 0 once the session is started).
.B wait
exits with the command's status (see
.BR wait ).
Other commands exit 0 on success, 1 on error (including
.B apply
refusing because of conflicts and
.B stop
failing to end a session) and 2 for a usage error.  When standard output
is closed early (as in
.BR "agentcracker ls | head \-1" ),
output stops quietly with status 141.
.SH EXAMPLES
The life of one session, from start to finish.
.SS Start an agent
.EX
$ cd ~/src/myproject
$ agentcracker claude
agentcracker: session 3f9a1c2e \(md 8 vCPU \(md 8.0G \(md net: Anthropic API only
\&...                              (Claude Code runs in the VM)
  M src/parser.rs
  A src/parser/tests.rs
  M Makefile  (runs or configures tools on the host \- review carefully)
3 files changed: 2 modified, 1 added
[d]iff [a]pply [s]hell [K]eep for later (not applied) [x] discard
.EE
.PP
Press
.B d
to read the diff,
.B a
to apply it, or Enter to decide later:
.EX
agentcracker: kept session 3f9a1c2e, changes NOT applied:
  agentcracker apply 3f9a1c2e | diff 3f9a1c2e | resume 3f9a1c2e | rm 3f9a1c2e
.EE
.SS Look at it later
.EX
$ agentcracker ls
ID         AGE  STATUS   CHANGES      DIRECTORY             COMMAND
3f9a1c2e   12m  exited   +1 ~2 \-0     ~/src/myproject       claude
$ agentcracker show
$ agentcracker diff | less
.EE
.SS Carry on where the agent stopped
The session's disk, the guest
.I $HOME
and the agent's history are all still there:
.EX
$ agentcracker resume claude \-\-continue
$ agentcracker shell          # look around yourself; exit when done
$ agentcracker resume make test
.EE
.SS See what it did
.EX
$ agentcracker log
15:42:01  bash      cargo build
15:42:03  net       DENY   crates.io:443
15:42:09  webfetch  https://docs.rs/erofs
15:42:09  net       ALLOW  docs.rs:443
15:42:12  websearch erofs extended inode layout  (server-side: not seen by the proxy)
15:42:20  edit      /home/alan/src/myproject/src/parser.rs
$ agentcracker log \-f 3f9a       # follow a running session
.EE
.SS Let it reach another host
.EX
$ agentcracker show | grep denied
network    crates.io                        3 denied
$ agentcracker resume \-\-allow crates.io \-\-allow static.crates.io cargo build
.EE
.SS Apply the work
.EX
$ agentcracker apply
  M src/parser.rs
  A src/parser/tests.rs
  M Makefile  (runs or configures tools on the host \- review carefully)
agentcracker: applied to ~/src/myproject: 3 written, 0 deleted
.EE
.PP
If a file was also edited on the host in the meantime:
.EX
These files changed on the host since the session started:
  src/parser.rs
[s]kip them and apply the rest, [o]verwrite them, or [c]ancel? s
.EE
.SS Take the agent's commits as a branch
.EX
$ agentcracker branch
agentcracker: created branch agentcracker/3f9a1c2e (2 commit(s) on main)
$ git log \-\-oneline main..agentcracker/3f9a1c2e
.EE
.SS Clean up
.EX
$ agentcracker rm 3f9a
removed 3f9a1c2e
.EE
.SS Scripted use
Without a terminal there is no menu: output goes to pipes and a summary
line ends the run.
.EX
$ agentcracker \-\-no\-net make test > test.log 2>&1; echo $?
.EE
.SS Several agents at once
Start each attempt in the background, wait for all of them, compare, and
apply the one you choose:
.EX
$ for t in tasks/*.md; do
>   agentcracker \-\-detach claude \-p "$(cat "$t")"
> done > ids
$ agentcracker wait $(cat ids)
3f9a1c2e exit 0
77b0d4e1 exit 0
c2a9f310 exit 1
$ for id in $(cat ids); do
>   agentcracker show \-\-json $id | jq \-c \(aq{id, exit_code, changes: .changes | del(.files)}\(aq
> done
$ agentcracker shell 77b0          # poke around in one (also while it runs)
$ agentcracker diff 77b0 | less
$ agentcracker apply 77b0 && agentcracker rm $(cat ids)
.EE
.PP
To end a background session early, keeping what it did so far:
.EX
$ agentcracker stop c2a9
stopped c2a9f310
.EE
.PP
Nothing is applied automatically: an orchestrating script or agent
prepares and compares, and applying stays a deliberate step.
.SH SECURITY
The agent cannot see the host's files outside the project, the user's
credentials (other than the agent's own), other repositories, or the
network beyond the allowlist, and a compromise of the guest kernel
stays inside the VM.  Firecracker itself runs as the invoking user under
a seccomp filter, without its jailer (which needs root).
.PP
The network allowlist limits where the VM connects, not what leaves it:
the Anthropic API accepts any API key, and server-side tools such as web
search run on Anthropic's servers, outside agentcracker's proxy, so an
agent steered by a malicious prompt can still send data out through
them.  Only
.B \-\-no\-net
closes those channels (and with them the agent's own API access).  Keep
secrets out of what the guest can see: tokens are stripped from remote
URLs in the snapshot's
.I .git/config
and registry credential files are left out of the toolchain snapshot,
and a new session warns about project files that look like credentials
(\fI.env\fR, private keys, ...), which
.B exclude
can leave out.
.PP
Nothing flows from the guest to the host except the change record, the
agent's transcripts and the network log.  Credentials are never copied
back (a token refreshed inside the VM is reported, not adopted), and
everything taken from the guest is printed with control and
bidirectional-override characters escaped, so a file name or diff cannot
rewrite the terminal while it is being reviewed.
.PP
What the agent writes is untrusted until reviewed: read the diff before
applying, especially entries flagged as running or configuring tools on
the host, and treat a branch imported with
.B branch
like any other unreviewed contribution.
